Abstract—Early Parkinson's Disease (PD) screening remains constrained by specialist dependency and subjective assessments. This paper presents NeuroLens, a cross-platform mobile application integrating four digital biomarker streams handwriting kinematics, voice acoustics, wearable tremor quantification, and cognitive assessment fused into a unified risk score. The system couples a React Native client with a Python FastAPI backend, Google Firestore, and a Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) wearable running a quantized 1D Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) via TinyML. Kinematic features are extracted from digitized spiral and wave drawing tasks (60 Hz, Gaussian-smoothed); voice analysis employs a dual-path acoustic-linguistic pipeline; and cognitive function is assessed via digitized Trail Making Test Part A (TMT-A) and Symbol Digit Modalities Test (SDMT). Individual modules were validated on separate datasets drawing (n=58), cognitive (n=4,267 PPMI), wearable (PhysioNet PADS), and voice (UCI PD speech corpus) and the fused score is presented as a proofof-concept requiring prospective unified validation. The platform additionally supports multilingual localization and longitudinal history tracking.
Keywords—Parkinson's Disease, TinyML, Multimodal Fusion, Digital Biomarkers, BLE Wearable, Kinematic Features, Voice Dysphonia, Cognitive Assessment, Mobile Health.
I. INTRODUCTION
Parkinson's Disease (PD) is the second most prevalent neurodegenerative disorder worldwide, affecting over 10 million people globally [1]. Clinical diagnosis is typically confirmed only after substantial dopaminergic neuron loss, making early detection critical. Conventional methods rely on subjective rating scales (e.g., UPDRS) and neuroimaging, which are resource-intensive and inaccessible in primary care or low-resource settings.
NeuroLens bridges this gap by capturing a comprehensive suite of motor and cognitive digital biomarkers within a single mobile application. Key contributions include: a multitask assessment protocol covering drawing, voice, wearable sensing, and cognitive tests; an on-device TinyML tremor classifier deployed to a BLE wearable enabling real-time offline inference; rigorous kinematic feature engineering with 60 Hz resampling and Gaussian smoothing; an AUCweighted late fusion mechanism producing a clinically interpretable global risk score; and a digitized cognitive battery (TMT-A and SDMT) validated against established neuropsychological instruments.
II. LITERATURE REVIEW
Digital PD screening has been pursued across four biomarker domains. Drotar et al. [2] established that kinematic parameters from digitized handwriting velocity, acceleration, and pen-pressure correlate strongly with UPDRS motor impairment, while Nomm et al. [9] validated spiral drawing as a sensitive and reproducible assessment. controls.
In the voice domain, Tsanas et al. [3] demonstrated that nonlinear acoustic dysphonia markers (RPDE, DFA, PPE) could discriminate PD from healthy controls with high accuracy; subsequent research further showed PD affects fluency, lexical access, and discourse structure [10].
For wearable sensing, Carignan et al. [4] showed IMUbased accelerometers could quantify tremors with clinicalgrade accuracy, and deep learning architectures on raw IMU signals have since outperformed hand-crafted pipelines [11]; however, deploying quantized neural networks directly onto microcontrollers the TinyML paradigm remains largely unexplored for PD-specific applications.
