In the cognitive domain, TMT-A and SDMT are validated as sensitive measures of psychomotor speed and early cognitive decline in PD [5], with digital implementations confirming that completion time and error rates reliably replicate paper-based equivalents [12].
Multimodal fusion approaches have consistently reported improved discriminative performance over any single modality [13], yet most existing platforms remain confined to offline research pipelines. NeuroLens addresses these gaps by unifying all four modalities through an AUC-weighted late fusion mechanism deployed across a mobile client, cloud backend, and BLE wearable edge device.
III. SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE
A. High-Level Overview
The NeuroLens architecture follows a client-cloud-edge pattern with three distinct tiers.
1) Mobile Client (Edge): A React Native application (ios and Android via Expo) handling user interactions, data capture, and BLE communication, with multilingual localization.
2) Cloud Backend (FastAPI): A Python microservice on Google Cloud responsible for ML inference orchestration, Firestore data persistence, and multimodal fusion. 3) BLE Wearable (Edge): A custom wrist-worn sensor a custom wrist-worn sensor running an on-device 1D CNN for real-time tremor classification over BLE GATT.
