IV. METHODOLOGY
A. Wearable-Based Tremor analysis (TinyML)
1) Hardware Design: The NeuroLens wearable is a custom wrist-worn device hosting an MPU-6050 IMU (100 Hz, ±2g accelerometer, ±250°/s gyroscope) and an ESP32 microcontroller. The 1D CNN is quantized to 8-bit integer precision using TensorFlow Lite for Microcontrollers, reducing model size to 3.7 KB with inference latency of 2.1 ms per window. The device exposes a GATT-based BLE service with two characteristics: command reception (UUID: 4fafc201-1fb5-459e-8fcc-c5c9c331914b) and result streaming(UUID:beb5483e-36e1-4688-b7f5ea07361b26a8).
2) Assessment Protocol: Three tremor categories are assessed sequentially Resting (CAT:0, arm fully supported), Postural (CAT:1, arm extended), and Kinetic (CAT:2, fingerto-nose task). Prior to capture, the mobile client transmits patient metadata: GEN:<0|1>, AGE:<int>, HAN:<0|1>, and CAT:<0|1|2>. Upon completion, the wearable returns DONE:<PD|HP>:<ratio>, where ratio denotes the proportion of high-risk windows.
3) Feature Extraction: Raw signals are segmented into non-overlapping 256-sample windows (2.56 s at 100 Hz). Gravity is removed via per-axis mean subtraction. Eight statistical and spectral features are extracted from each of the accelerometer and gyroscope, yielding 16 sensor features. Three demographic features (age, sex, handedness) and three one-hot task-category flags are appended, producing a final 22-feature input vector (Table I). All numeric features are standard-scaled on-device using pre-computed constants embedded in the model header.
