The continuous output of the CNN is a sequence of probabilities for a standardized assessment session comprising 12 consecutive windows (approximately 30 seconds). To eliminate bias, the optimal classification thresholds were mathematically derived during 5-fold Group K-Fold cross-validation.
a) Window-Level Classification: The optimal threshold t_w = 0.560 is derived by maximizing Youden's J statistic from the ROC curve:
t_w = argmax(TPR - FPR)
Each window is assigned to a binary high-risk flag (w_i = 1 if probability ≥ t_w, else 0).
b) Session-Level Aggregation: The proportion of highrisk windows R = Σw_i / N is compared against t_s = 0.44, derived via exhaustive grid search maximizing patient-level balanced accuracy:
t_s = argmax [ (Sensitivity_s + Specificity_s) / 2 ]
This two-tier temporal aggregation mitigates transient sensor noise, boosting balanced accuracy from 66.0% (window-level) to 70.2% (session-level), with held-out sensitivity of 74.3% and specificity of 65.8%. Bootstrap resampling (n = 10,000) yields 95% CI of [63.8%–75.4%] for balanced accuracy and [0.694–0.816] for AUC of 0.732. Session-level aggregation was specifically designed to preserve sensitivity, as a missed PD case carries greater clinical cost than a false referral.
B. Drawing-Based Motor Analysis
1) Task Protocol and Data Acquisition Interface: Subjects perform two sequential tasks on a custom touchscreen interface. In the Spiral Task, participants trace a two-cycle Archimedean spiral, evaluating continuous motor coordination, spatial memory, and sustained curvature under mild cognitive load. In the Wave Task, subjects draw a continuous sinusoidal waveform left to right, isolating rhythmic fluid motion and frequency-dependent motor limitations. Raw touch events are logged as spatiotemporal tuples (x, y, t) at millisecond precision.
2) Signal Pre-processing:Raw touch sequences are resampled to a uniform 60 Hz grid via cubic spline interpolation to compensate for OS scheduling jitter. Gaussian smoothing (σ = 2 samples, ≈33 ms) attenuates digitizer quantization noise while preserving kinematic features below 15 Hz. Velocity and acceleration trajectories are derived via central finite differences.
3) Feature Engineering and Multimodal Fusion: Conditioned trajectories are quantified across two complementary feature domains, capturing both spatial accuracy and temporal motor dynamics.
a) Geometric (Spatial) Features: Extracted directly from the coordinate matrix, these features quantify the geometrical accuracy. Key metrics include the standard deviation of curvature, standard deviation of radial deviation from the ideal spiral, and the root mean square error (RMSE) between the traced y-trajectory and a smoothed ideal baseline.
b) Kinematic (Temporal) Features: Derived from firstand second-order temporal derivatives, these features quantify micro-hesitations, rigidity, and bradykinesia. Core metrics are: (i) the Coefficient of Variation (CV) of velocity magnitude (σv/μv); (ii) the Pause Ratio, defined as the fraction of elapsed time below the 10th percentile of nominal velocity; and (iii) the CV of multidimensional jerk magnitude, characterizing acceleration smoothness.
Spatial and kinematic feature arrays are horizontally concatenated into task-specific data matrices. Subject age (continuous) and biological sex (categorical) are fused into the kinematic feature space to control for non-pathological age-related motor stiffness. Recursive Feature Elimination (RFE) subsequently isolates the most discriminative markers, yielding a final 7-dimensional feature vector comprising the six highest-ranked kinematic/spatial features and subject age
