C. Speech & Vocal Biomarker Analysis
1) Mobile Application and Data Collection: The voice component non-invasively assesses neurological and motor functions through guided prompts designed to elicit voice tremors, micro-hesitations, and cognitive-linguistic variations. Audio is recorded and preprocessed to a standardized 16-bit, 22.05 kHz mono WAV format via FFmpeg. The module is trained and validated on the UCI PD speech dataset (195 voice recordings), split 80/20 at the subject level using stratified sampling by diagnosis to prevent data leakage.
2) Acoustic Feature Analysis: The acoustic pipeline captures involuntary tremors and amplitude variations associated with PD. a) Feature Extraction: Acoustic features are extracted using Praat/Parselmouth. A total of 18 parameters are derived, including fundamental frequencies ( !! , !"# , !$! ), frequency perturbations (Jitter: %, Abs, RAP, PPQ5, DDP), amplitude perturbations (Shimmer: %, APQ3, APQ5, APQ11, DDA), noise measures (HNR, NHR), and nonlinear dynamic features (DFA, RPDE, D2). Engineered features such as instability ratio (Jitter × Shimmer) and signal quality index are also computed. b) Preprocessing: Outliers are handled using the Interquartile Range (IQR) method. Multicollinear and lowvariance features are removed to improve model stability. Class imbalance is addressed using SMOTE applied to the training data, followed by normalization using StandardScaler. c) Model Training: Machine learning models including Support Vector Machines (SVM), Random Forest, XGBoost, and Multilayer Perceptrons (MLP) are trained. Hyperparameter tuning is performed using GridSearchCV with 10-fold StratifiedKFold cross-validation. Feature selection prioritizes the most informative attributes.
3) Linguistic Feature Analysis :Parkinson’s Disease (PD) affects cognitive and language functions, resulting in reduced lexical diversity and syntactic complexity.
a) Transcription and Extraction : Speech recordings are transcribed using an Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) model. Linguistic features are extracted, including fluency metrics (words per sentence, sentence length variability), lexical diversity (TTR, MTLD, HDD), syntactic complexity
(POS ratios, parse tree depth), disfluency patterns (repetitions, filler words, pause frequency), and readability measures (Flesch–Kincaid index).
b) Preprocessing and Training: Similar preprocessing steps, including IQR-based outlier handling, normalization using StandardScaler, and stratified data splitting, are applied. Models such as SVM, Random Forest, XGBoost, and Logistic Regression are optimized using GridSearchCV with cross-validation.
