The final multimodal risk score is computed as a weighted arithmetic mean of normalized output probabilities from each subsystem, strictly weighted by per-module AUC. The drawing module carries the highest weight, reflecting strong kinematic biomarkers in Parkinsonian micrographia; the cognitive module captures bradyphrenia; the wearable reflects 30-second patient-level TinyML generalization; and the voice module contributes its acoustic-linguistic benchmark.
$$R_{final} = \frac{\sum (P_i \times AUC_i)}{\sum AUC_i}$$
This late-fusion architecture provides three critical advantages for edge-to-cloud telehealth: (1) Modularity, allowing individual machine learning models to be updated or swapped without retraining the entire pipeline; (2) Robustness to missing data, as the denominator dynamically adjusts if a patient only completes a subset of the assessments. (3) Clinical Interpretability, as the contribution of each modality to the final risk score remains deterministic and proportional to its established predictive power.
Several practical limitations should be acknowledged before clinical deployment. Training data domain shift may
reduce performance across different populations and devices; touch and IMU hardware variability across smartphones and wearables affects consistency; and the AUC fusion weights are derived from datasets of vastly different sizes, making direct comparison imperfect. The platform is intended as a screening support tool for specialist referral only, not a standalone diagnostic instrument.
VII. CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK
NeuroLens presents a comprehensive platform for early PD screening, combining four biomarker domains within a single accessible mobile application. Key technical innovations include the TinyML on-device tremor classifier, the 60 Hz kinematic resampling pipeline, the dual-path voice analysis engine, and the SHAP-enhanced cognitive module, all unified by AUC-weighted late fusion. Prospective clinical validation is required before deployment.
Future work will focus on: (1) prospective unified validation with ≥500 subjects completing all four modalities; (2) calibration of fusion thresholds using multi-center data; (3) real-world usability studies across diverse demographic groups; (4) integration of PPG-based heart rate variability for autonomic dysfunction assessment; and (5) federated learning to enable model updates without compromising patient data privacy.
